rota lap N was 4091 at the open by git rev-list --count HEAD, so N mod 5 is row 1 -- AIR, Saturn, law and boundary, sense of touch. My own previous lap read row 0 by hand, so the count and the hand agree for once. Read the threshold and all three seats: TAME Core (cardinal), Single-Stranded (fixed), Gauge Style's quality-assurance section (dual). The threshold named this lap before it had a subject: a claim of a boundary is tested by pressing on it, and if the hand passes through, the boundary was a wish.
think THE ROUND OPENED ON A CORPSE AND THE CORPSE WAS CARRYING SOMETHING. git stash list showed six entries, newest at 20260906-165213, about forty minutes old. Per %321 a dead lap leaves no dirty index -- its leavings are stashed -- so the open reads the stash rather than the tree. stash@{0} held four files and 187 insertions: reds_citation_scan.sh rewritten, its control grown to 52 cases, its witness, and a complete ledger row. None of it was in HEAD.
think I CHECKED WHETHER IT HAD ALREADY LANDED BEFORE TRUSTING IT. Compared each file's blob in the stash against HEAD's: all four differed. Then read the diff to see whether the difference was substance or drift -- it was substance, the FOLD citation form widened from a bare number to the sigilled spelling the ledger's own law seats. HEAD carried reach 66 of 274; the stash carried 218 of 282.
think AND THEN THE ACTUAL FINDING, WHICH WAS THE BOX ITSELF. stash_record_scan.sh exists precisely to report a round-open stash holding work nothing carries. I ran it: stashes=6 records=5 landed=5 unlanded=0 parked=0 verdict=ok. Six stashes, five read, verdict clean -- over a stash holding a finished feature. The scan filters every stashed path through ^session-logs/, so a lap that dies BEFORE writing its log leaves nothing the reading can place. The log is written last, so that is the ordinary way a lap dies.
think THIS IS THE THIRD FIRING OF ONE FAMILY IN ONE DAY AND THE THIRD IS THE SHARPEST. %505 was a guard that printed only what it gated. %507 was a park branch counted as a landing. Both were about reach. This one is about SUBJECT: %507 widened WHICH REFS count as a landing, which is the right question asked of the wrong set, because the gap was a stash with no record to stand anywhere at all. A filter is a claim about a subject, and a guard whose filter is narrower than its box reports on the box.
think THE REPAIR REFUSED TO BE A GATE, AND THE SCAN'S OWN HEADER ARGUED IT FOR ME. It already says a meter satisfied by deleting evidence teaches deletion. A recordless stash cannot be cleared by writing a record -- the record would be outside the stash -- so a gate on it is cleared only by dropping the stash, which destroys the very bytes the reading exists to surface. So silent and silent_files are REPORTED, the stash named by line in every mode, and the witness gates the PRESENCE of the reading rather than its value. Read as reach, in the same voice unread_links uses one guard over -- which I landed in the same commit, which is a pleasing accident rather than a plan.
think PROVEN FROM BOTH SIDES, TWICE, BECAUSE ONE SIDE IS NOT PROOF. Planted the counter that never rises: control went 45 pass to 44 pass 1 fail. Then planted the subtle one -- held_record set AFTER the dedupe continue rather than before it -- which manufactures a false silence out of two stashes sharing one record, and leg 46 bit that too. Both restored, witness GREEN.
obs THE ROSTER'S RED WAS MINE AND MECHANICAL. index_row_bound refused on two stamps standing twice on the day shelf, each stale copy naming a REDS number the ledger had since renumbered past -- 20260906.153324 read %507 in one row and %505 in the other, and the ledger says %505. %440's family, repaired by hand for the ninth time. Traced each stamp to its row before deleting, rather than keeping the newer-looking one.
obs MY OWN ROW NUMBER MOVED AT THE FETCH, WHICH IS THE FIFTH TIME IN A DAY. The stash booked %508 at 16:44:37; the fetch found a peer had published %508 at 17:31:41 -- 24 seconds after my lap opened. Published numbers never move and mine had never reached the spine, so mine renumbered to %510 and the new finding took %511. Six stale self-citations of %506 inside the recovered files went with it -- the dead lap had already been renumbered once mid-flight.
obs TWO GUARDS READ RED COLD AND GREEN ALONE. amphora_restore and amphora_restore_negative both refused in the cold pass and both passed when re-run minutes later, untouched. Eight ships share a 4-core pier and every seat runs tier lap, so the leading read is contention -- yet nothing on this tree distinguishes contention from a flake, and I am not claiming one. Recorded rather than diagnosed.
obs THE NEW GUARD'S FIRST LIVE SUBJECT WAS THIS LAP'S OWN FOLD. Folding to a shelf wrote a recital line reading `Row %511 folded to`, sigilled -- exactly the spelling the elder form could not see. Citation reading went 192 to 193 links and agreed. A guard proving itself on the work that landed it is the cheapest possible confirmation and I will take it.
obs THE QA FRAME I PUSHED WAS WORTH WORKING. The landed-accounts shelf read C+/77, and the reason was service: living_citers=0, named_by_card=no. I had folded my own account onto a shelf and pointed nothing at it -- which is precisely the 82-of-607 defect I keep reporting to Keaton, committed by my own hand inside the lap that reports it. Named it on the card, paid the bytes out of my own block, re-graded B/84.
obs THE CARD IS AT 40,954 OF 40,960 AND THAT IS SIX BYTES OF SLACK. Folding my elder account bought 1,706 bytes and the new one cost 1,700 after four passes of trimming. Per-ship budget is 2,048 and I am inside it; the pin is over because eight blocks are. Worth Keaton's word rather than more trimming.
